# Coding standards

The reviewer applies these to every Haskell change. `scripts/check` already enforces formatting,
warnings, type signatures, imports, extensions and the banned functions, so they are not repeated
here.

The reader is {{reader}}. Every rule serves that reader.

## Plain Haskell

Use data types, records, pattern matching, `Maybe`, `Either` and `IO`, plus the approved extras
below. Type-level programming, lens, monad transformers, effect libraries and new type classes
need the owner's approval first. Once approved, they go into the list.

Approved extras:

- None.

Instances of library classes are fine, for example `Show` or `ToJSON`.

## Comments

A reader can follow a module from top to bottom by its comments alone.

- Every module starts with a `-- |` comment that says what the module is for.
- Every top-level definition has a `-- |` comment that says what it is for and why.
- Explain a Haskell construct briefly the first time a module uses it, if the reader may not know
  it. For example `<$>`, `traverse` or a `where` block. One short sentence, sometimes with the
  equivalent in a language the reader knows.
- Plain English and short sentences. No filler, no praise of the code, no restating the code in
  words.

## Text and errors

- Use `Text` for text. `String` only where a library forces it.
- Expected failures are values: return `Either` with the module's error type. Exceptions are for
  the unexpected only, such as a full disk.

## Functions and names

- Functions are short, do one thing and read top to bottom. Pull out a named helper instead of
  nesting.
- Write explicit arguments and lambdas. Composing two or three functions with `.` is fine.
- Names say what a value is: `environment`, `argument`. Single letters only in tiny lambdas.
- Use the same name for the same concept everywhere: in code, tests and docs.

## Dependencies

A new entry in `build-depends` in `{{package}}.cabal` needs the owner's approval.

## Tests

- New behaviour comes with a test. A bug fix comes with a test that was red before the fix.
- Test names describe behaviour.
